    27	
    28	This is not only a `new` defect. There are five record-family/call-site rows
    29	that mint sequential ids, implemented by four scan bodies in three modules:
    30	
    31	| Record | Production allocator | Present behaviour |
    32	| --- | --- | --- |
    33	| opus `W-*` | `packages/cli/src/new.ts:53` (`newItem`) | scans local `opera/`; allocates at line 57 |
    34	| opus `W-*` | `packages/cli/src/new.ts:170` (`runNew`) | duplicates that local scan; allocates at line 174 |
    35	| lesson `L-*` | `packages/cli/src/retro.ts:381` (`draftRetro`, helper at line 74) | scans local `lessons/`, then carries an in-memory offset at line 383 because lessons are written later |
    36	| petitio `P-*` | `packages/cli/src/retro.ts:430` (`draftRetro`, same helper) | scans local `petitiones/` |
    37	| petitio `P-*` | `packages/commands/src/talk.ts:173` (`openPetitio`) | independently scans local `petitiones/`; allocates at line 177 |
    38	
    39	`packages/commands/src/writes.ts:616` (`runAnswer`) does **not** allocate: it resolves
    40	and edits an existing petitio. Nor is there a production `D-*` allocator.
    41	Decision records are read and validated; `talk`'s `ACTUM:` path writes a
    42	date-and-slug acta entry (`packages/commands/src/talk.ts:201`, with a
    43	filename collision suffix at line 207), not `decisions/D-*.md`. That suffix
    44	is outside the sequential record-id families. The ref-wide maxima measured
    45	today are `P-013`, `L-038`, and `D-028`; the last is measurement, not a new
    46	decision-writing scope.
    47	
    48	Replace every real sequential allocator above with one shared implementation
    49	in `@bisellium/commands`. The helper owns both discovery and the exclusive
    50	creation attempt; callers supply the officina root, record directory, prefix,
    51	and a renderer for the bytes. Keeping the write in the helper is necessary:
    52	returning a supposedly free string to a later ordinary `writeFileSync` would
    53	retain the race this opus is meant to close.
    54	
    55	## The id universe
    56	
    57	Ids remain separate families. A `W-*` allocation takes the maximum numeric
    58	suffix among `W-*.md` in that officina's `opera/`; `P-*` uses its
    59	`petitiones/`, and `L-*` its `lessons/`. A high `P-*` must not advance a
    60	`W-*`: the prefixes name different record kinds, their directories have
    61	different contracts, and existing ids already overlap numerically.
    62	
    63	For one family, the candidate maximum is the union of:
    64	
    65	1. exact matching filenames in the requested directory of the current
    66	   working tree, including untracked and otherwise uncommitted records; and
    67	2. exact matching paths in the same officina directory in every existing
    68	   `refs/heads/*` and `refs/remotes/*` ref.
    69	
    70	The implementation finds the repository containing the officina, computes
    71	the officina's repository-relative path, obtains refs with the read-only
    72	equivalent of:
    73	
    74	```text
    75	git for-each-ref --format=%(refname) refs/heads refs/remotes
    76	```
    77	
    78	and reads each relevant tree with `git ls-tree`, also read-only. Commands use
    79	argument arrays, not a shell-built command line. Only a basename exactly
    80	matching `^<prefix>-(\d+)\.md$` contributes. File contents and front matter
    81	do not: the filename is the collision being prevented.
    82	
    83	Canonicalize the officina root and Git worktree root with `realpath` before
    84	computing the relative path. Discover Git from that officina, not the process
    85	cwd; linked worktrees use their own worktree root, not the common Git directory.
    86	This keeps symlink aliases from silently selecting a different tree path.
    87	
    88	The repository-relative officina path is part of the lookup. For
    89	`examples/sample-studio`, refs are inspected at
    90	`examples/sample-studio/opera/`, never at `studio/opera/`. Its ref-wide opus
    91	maximum is `W-007`, so it allocates `W-008` even though the same repository's
    92	real officina reaches `W-109`. This is the necessary meaning of "same
    93	officina on every ref", and prevents the fixture from inheriting production
    94	bookkeeping merely because both live in one Git repository.
    95	
    96	Tags, stash, refs outside `refs/heads/*` and `refs/remotes/*` (including
    97	`refs/pull/*`), and an upstream server not represented locally are outside
    98	the universe. Every ref inside the two required namespaces contributes,
    99	including any cached queue or pull-request ref there; no name-based exclusions.
   100	
   101	### Git degradation and stale remote state
   102	
   103	Allocation stays usable when `git` cannot be executed or the officina is not
   104	inside a repository. If repository discovery, ref enumeration, or tree
   105	inspection fails, the helper falls back to the working-tree directory scan
   106	and writes a clear warning to stderr saying that ref-wide allocation was
   107	unavailable and local records alone were used. It does not throw merely
   108	because Git is unavailable. The caller's existing result/exit-code contract
   109	otherwise remains unchanged.
   110	
   111	This chooses filing availability over ref-wide uniqueness during Git failure;
   112	the warning must not claim that the degraded id is unused on other refs.
   113	Discard partial ref results on any such failure so the fallback has one
   114	testable meaning. Filesystem scan/create errors are not Git degradation.
   115	
   116	The allocator never runs `git fetch`, implicitly or otherwise. Fetch is a
   117	network mutation, would make an ordinary local filing depend on credentials
   118	and availability, and still could not provide a global lock. Remote-tracking
   119	refs are a cache: the helper uses the cache present when invoked. If it is
   120	stale, ids known only to the server are necessarily invisible. Operators who
   121	need fresher knowledge fetch explicitly before filing.
   122	
   123	### Concurrent writers in one checkout
   124	
   125	After discovering maximum `N`, the helper renders `N + 1` and creates the
   126	record itself with `writeFileSync(target, bytes, { flag: "wx" })`, following
   127	the non-overwrite pattern at `packages/commands/src/verdict.ts:181`. On
   128	`EEXIST`, it increments and tries the next id; it never overwrites, truncates,
   129	or treats the collision as success. Other I/O errors propagate to the
   130	caller's established error handling. This closes the same-checkout race
   131	without a stale lock file.
   132	
   133	The guarantee is deliberately bounded: exclusive create coordinates
   134	processes that share a filesystem checkout. Separate worktrees can still
   135	race after observing the same ref set; Git refs are evidence, not a
   136	distributed reservation service. Once either filing becomes a visible ref,
   137	later allocations advance past it.
   138	
   139	`runNew --brief` reserves the opus record exclusively before writing its
   140	companion brief, and the brief is also a non-overwriting `wx` write. If the
   141	brief write fails, the newly created opus record from this invocation is
   142	removed before reporting failure, so the command does not leave a `spec:`
   143	pointer to an absent or pre-existing brief. `newItem` has no companion file.
   144	Retro writes each allocated lesson through the shared helper as it is
   145	allocated; its recurrence calculation continues to use the history snapshot
   146	taken before this retro, so its meaning does not change. Petitiones from
   147	retro and talk use the same helper and collision retry.
   148	Recurrence still combines that snapshot with this invocation's new lesson,
   149	exactly as `packages/cli/src/retro.ts:402` does today; it does not reread the
   150	newly written lessons into history. Retro is not an all-or-nothing transaction.
   151	
   152	## Interfaces
   153	
   154	Add one commands-package module with one production seam, named for ids rather
   155	than for any caller. Its effective contract is:
   156	
   157	```ts
   158	createNextRecord(
   159	  studioRoot: string,
   160	  directory: "opera" | "petitiones" | "lessons",
   161	  prefix: "W" | "P" | "L",
   162	  render: (id: string) => string | Uint8Array,
   163	): { id: string; path: string };
   164	```
   165	
   166	The exact type names may follow house style, but discovery plus exclusive
   167	write must remain one operation and callers must not regain a scan/write
   168	gap. The helper returns the absolute created path so `runNew --brief` can
   169	perform its bounded rollback without reconstructing it. It may be generic
   170	enough for a future `D-*` writer, but this opus adds no decision verb and no
   171	unused decision-specific branch.
   172	
   173	The helper may synchronously invoke Git because every caller is synchronous
   174	today. It must not print in the normal Git-backed case. Its only direct user
   175	output is the stderr degradation warning; existing caller stdout stays byte
   176	compatible (`W-NNN`, `petitio P-NNN opened`, and existing retro results).
   177	
   178	## Files owned
   179	
   180	- `packages/commands/src/ids.ts` — shared ref-aware, exclusive-create
     1	/**
     2	 * Shared sequential-record allocator.  The filename is the id claim: find
     3	 * the highest matching filename in this checkout and every locally-known
     4	 * branch/ref, then reserve the next candidate with an exclusive create.
     5	 */
     6	import { execFileSync } from "node:child_process";
     7	import { lstatSync, mkdirSync, readdirSync, realpathSync, writeFileSync } from "node:fs";
     8	import { join, relative, sep } from "node:path";
     9	
    10	export type RecordDirectory = "opera" | "petitiones" | "lessons";
    11	export type RecordPrefix = "W" | "P" | "L";
    12	
    13	export interface CreatedRecord {
    14	  id: string;
    15	  path: string;
    16	}
    17	
    18	const GIT_OUTPUT_LIMIT = 16 * 1024 * 1024;
    19	// Record suffixes are bounded to nine decimal digits; 999,999,999 is final.
    20	const MAX_SUFFIX_DIGITS = 9;
    21	const MAX_SUFFIX = 999_999_999;
    22	// A single allocation may absorb at most 100 concurrent EEXIST collisions.
    23	const MAX_EEXIST_RETRIES = 100;
    24	
    25	function numericSuffix(name: string, pattern: RegExp): number | undefined {
    26	  const match = pattern.exec(name);
    27	  const digits = match?.[1];
    28	  if (digits === undefined || digits.length > MAX_SUFFIX_DIGITS) return undefined;
    29	  const suffix = Number(digits);
    30	  return Number.isSafeInteger(suffix) && suffix <= MAX_SUFFIX ? suffix : undefined;
    31	}
    32	
    33	function localMaximum(directoryPath: string, pattern: RegExp): number {
    34	  let maximum = 0;
    35	  for (const name of readdirSync(directoryPath)) {
    36	    const suffix = numericSuffix(name, pattern);
    37	    if (suffix !== undefined) maximum = Math.max(maximum, suffix);
    38	  }
    39	  return maximum;
    40	}
    41	
    42	function gitOutput(cwd: string, args: string[]): string {
    43	  return execFileSync("git", ["-C", cwd, ...args], {
    44	    encoding: "utf8",
    45	    env: {
    46	      ...process.env,
    47	      // Tree discovery must be local, literal and independent of refs/replace.
    48	      GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH: "1",
    49	      GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS: "1",
    50	      GIT_NO_REPLACE_OBJECTS: "1",
    51	    },
    52	    maxBuffer: GIT_OUTPUT_LIMIT,
    53	    stdio: ["ignore", "pipe", "pipe"],
    54	  });
    55	}
    56	
    57	/**
    58	 * Returns the ref-wide maximum, or `undefined` after any Git failure.  The
    59	 * result is all-or-nothing so a failed tree read cannot leave a misleading
    60	 * partial maximum in use.
    61	 */
    62	function refMaximum(studioRoot: string, directory: RecordDirectory, pattern: RegExp): number | undefined {
    63	  try {
    64	    const discoveredRoot = gitOutput(studioRoot, ["rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"]).trim();
    65	    const physicalRepo = realpathSync(discoveredRoot);
    66	    const studioRelative = relative(physicalRepo, studioRoot);
    67	    if (studioRelative === ".." || studioRelative.startsWith(`..${sep}`)) throw new Error("officina is outside its Git worktree");
    68	
    69	    const gitDirectory = [...(studioRelative ? studioRelative.split(sep) : []), directory].join("/");
    70	    const refs = gitOutput(physicalRepo, [
    71	      "for-each-ref",
    72	      "--format=%(refname)",
    73	      "refs/heads",
    74	      "refs/remotes",
    75	    ])
    76	      .split(/\r?\n/)
    77	      .filter((ref) => ref.length > 0);
    78	
    79	    let maximum = 0;
    80	    for (const ref of refs) {
    81	      const paths = gitOutput(physicalRepo, ["ls-tree", "-r", "-z", "--name-only", ref, "--", gitDirectory]).split("\0");
    82	      const directoryPrefix = `${gitDirectory}/`;
    83	      for (const path of paths) {
    84	        if (!path.startsWith(directoryPrefix)) continue;
    85	        const name = path.slice(directoryPrefix.length);
    86	        if (name.includes("/")) continue;
    87	        const suffix = numericSuffix(name, pattern);
    88	        if (suffix !== undefined) maximum = Math.max(maximum, suffix);
    89	      }
    90	    }
    91	    return maximum;
    92	  } catch {
    93	    return undefined;
    94	  }
    95	}
    96	
    97	function requireRealDirectory(path: string): void {
    98	  const stat = lstatSync(path);
    99	  if (stat.isSymbolicLink() || !stat.isDirectory()) {
   100	    throw new Error(`record directory must be a real directory, not a symbolic link: ${path}`);
   101	  }
   102	}
   103	
   104	export function createNextRecord(
   105	  studioRoot: string,
   106	  directory: RecordDirectory,
   107	  prefix: RecordPrefix,
   108	  render: (id: string) => string | Uint8Array,
   109	): CreatedRecord {
   110	  const physicalStudio = realpathSync(studioRoot);
   111	  const directoryPath = join(physicalStudio, directory);
   112	  mkdirSync(directoryPath, { recursive: true });
   113	  requireRealDirectory(directoryPath);
   114	  const pattern = new RegExp(`^${prefix}-(\\d+)\\.md$`);
   115	  const local = localMaximum(directoryPath, pattern);
   116	  const fromRefs = refMaximum(physicalStudio, directory, pattern);
   117	  if (fromRefs === undefined) {
   118	    console.error("warning: Git ref-wide allocation unavailable; using local records only");
   119	  }
   120	
   121	  let candidate = Math.max(local, fromRefs ?? 0) + 1;
   122	  for (let collisions = 0; ; collisions++) {
   123	    if (candidate > MAX_SUFFIX) {
   124	      throw new Error(`record id suffix exhausted at ${MAX_SUFFIX_DIGITS} digits for prefix ${prefix}`);
   125	    }
   126	    const id = `${prefix}-${String(candidate).padStart(3, "0")}`;
   127	    const path = join(directoryPath, `${id}.md`);
   128	    try {
   129	      const contents = render(id);
   130	      requireRealDirectory(directoryPath);
   131	      writeFileSync(path, contents, { flag: "wx" });
   132	      return { id, path };
   133	    } catch (error) {
   134	      if ((error as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code !== "EEXIST") throw error;
   135	      if (collisions + 1 >= MAX_EEXIST_RETRIES) {
   136	        throw new Error(`record allocation stopped after ${MAX_EEXIST_RETRIES} EEXIST collisions for prefix ${prefix}`);
   137	      }
   138	      candidate++;
   139	    }
   140	  }
   141	}
